A||N||14||bHooper||Removing road grime from suspension parts||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||10-04-2000||01:28 PM||||I am going to replace my front shocks and end links, tommorow night, and was wondering what was a good product to remove road grime from the suspension.  <P>Last year, I tried some standered cleaner and a pressure washer, but that didn't work very well.  I was thinking of trying Easy Off or some other oven cleaner, this time.  Seems like I have heard something about that working right.<P>All advise is welcome.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||10-04-2000||01:40 PM||||Best thing I've ever seen is some stuff called "Mean Green". Kind of like Simple Green, only works better, IMHO. The only place I have ever seen it is in Dollar General stores. <BR>The instructions on the bottle say NOT to use it on aluminum. <BR>I used it to clean many years of crud off my doorjambs and such, when I was getting my Firebird ready to sell. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||10-04-2000||02:42 PM||||"I was thinking of trying Easy Off or some other oven cleaner, this time."<P>My question to you is how much do you like paint?  Oven cleaner eats off paint!  at least in my expierences.  <P>Mark Johnson ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||10-04-2000||02:52 PM||||Wouldn't ol' Engine degreaser work on the grimey areas?  Maybe keep it off the rubber brake lines just in case?  Let it soak then power wash it off.  Just an idea.||||reg||5
Z||000004||bHooper||10-04-2000||03:27 PM||||i think i tried the engine degreaser.  the problem is that there is 15 years of road grime built up on the ball joints/tie rod ends/end links, etc. that i need to get rid of, b4 i work on the car tommorow night.<P>there really isnt any paint to worry about in these areas.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||10-04-2000||03:39 PM||||I use SuperClean with pretty good results.  It is put out by Castrol and can be found at WalMart.  I use it full strength.  Let it soak for a while before washing it off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-04-2000||03:43 PM||||I was cleaning up the wire harness from the C-500 plug to the engine (previous owner unwrapped it and left it like a rats nest) and found that WD-40 worked best in wiping away the road grime from the wires.  SuperClean did a fair job but WD-40 worked the best.||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||10-04-2000||04:20 PM||||If you have an air compressor, get a suction blowgun and use mineral spirits to clean these parts.<P>Works very well on accumulated hardened grease deposits.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||10-04-2000||04:50 PM||||I use simple green and a heavy duty scrub brush and mine came pretty clean.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||10-04-2000||05:04 PM||||guess im going to have to get filthy dirty, one way or another.<P>the mineral spirits and blow gun is a great idea... kinda like getting ink out of a t-shirt/fabric, where it has been miss printed.<P>i will check into that today.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||ROB MAC||10-04-2000||06:08 PM||||And remember, if it eats through grime and tar on the car it'll probably eat through your driveway so you might wanna do it at someone elses house.||||reg||1
Z||000011||hugh||10-04-2000||07:12 PM||||If you use anything with a blow gun,DISCONNECT THE BATTERY!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-04-2000||07:19 PM||||Simple/Mean green, CitriSolv, diluted liquid laundry soap, anything like that with a stiff nylon brush should take it right off. Goes faster is you knock the heavy stuff off with the end of a stick or flat blade screw driver. There's really no need to use strong solvents of any type. It's not like the stuff is cooked on there.||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||10-04-2000||08:02 PM||||well i spent a hour or so knocking off all the heavy dirt with a screw driver, chisel and a really worn out wire brush.<P>i bet i knocked a good pound or two of dirt off of the suspension!<P>after looking at it in more detail, it does look like a good stiff brush and detergent will do the job nicely.  the most difficult spots are around the greas fittings.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||10-04-2000||11:06 PM||||Try to be careful not to push crap into the tip of the grease fittings. the check ball doesn't always have the hole sealed very well. Any dirt in the fitting will eventually end up inside the joint.||||reg||1
